# Study S007 — Cook, categorical models of logics (R&D tracker)

Coordinate: 20.6.7/7.6.11/6.6.8
Issued: 2026-11-11 20:55 CST
Request (from session record): Will posted John D. Cook's post "Categorical models of logics" (2026-10-12) in MOAT; filed as Source Study S007 under R&D tracker charter rule 12 (grounds held concepts; no Novelty Index; the Research Oracle does not move). Scope included the two sibling posts the main post links: "topological models of modal logic" (2026-10-04) and "Privacy policies and modal logic" (2026-10-08).
Status: completed — study filed in research.phext Chapter 23 + phextura shadow transaction validated GREEN

Notes:
- Canonical: Chapter 23 (23.1.1 source record, 23.1.2 delta, 23.1.3 author check, 23.2.1 synthesis, 23.3.1 standoff). Cluster S founded (logic as structure): concepts 1.2.171-1.2.175 (170 -> 175). Foundations 1.3.185-1.3.188 (category; topology; modal logic; the tenses); defined words 1.3.189-1.3.193 (morphism, functor, coproduct, intuitionistic, bicartesian), coined in singular-coined.txt; names barth, datta, johndcook, lambek, logics, nissenbaum declared in singular-names.txt ('logics' enters only inside the named work's title, rule 10(b)).
- Grounding verdicts: 1.2.40 SUPPORTED and MADE SHARPER; 1.2.42 MADE SHARPER; 1.2.41 SUPPORTED; 1.2.48 sharpened by kinship; 1.2.87 SUPPORTED with the approval/receipt comparison opened as Q71. Absent before the study: any held concept on categories, functors, modal logic, or topological semantics. Questions Q69 (display-policy functor), Q70 (identity events for standing-still), Q71 (approval and receipt as one operator) opened.
- Findings (23.2.1, filed as findings, not charter amendments): (a) the standing record reads as a category — standings as objects, receipt-carrying legal transitions (1.4.6) as morphisms, chained receipts as the proof a later standing stands from an earlier; the chain proves the move, not worldly truth, and the record writes no identity event for standing-still (Q70). (b) phextura's warrant display policy should be required to be a functor, not merely a function (audit finding L4): composition and identity preservation are the content; the converter's current warrant-view maps one edge at a time. (c) the policy essay's tense operators name the standing record's past-tense distinctions, and its joint-satisfiability lesson pressures the registry's per-entry checking — the joint question is unwritten.
- State machine review (rule 13): none present in the material — recorded in one line at 23.1.2, 23.2.1, and as a swept entry at 1.5.2; none invented.
- Author check (rule 15): three posts read entire, no phext signature terms; web search found no tie; Will's Twitter archive holds 4 retweets of @JohnDCook (2012-2013) and 1 reply from Will to Cook (2013-03-03) — a reader's tie, one way and old. No evidence Cook knows phext or Will's work; no use without naming found. Record at 23.1.3 and 1.5.6 (researcher count 96 -> 97; checks run 13 -> 14).
- Registry FLAGGED, not changed (per brief): the study's 19 new scrolls are claim-bearing and enter the registry at the next drawing — 5 concepts (1.2.171-175) Unknown/attributed; 4 foundations + 5 defined words (1.3.185-193) True/stipulation; 23.1.1 True/verified; 23.1.3 True/verified (override precedent); 23.2.1 Unknown/synthesis; 23.1.2 and 23.3.1 per extract_registry.py kind defaults. Until then totality lags: 544 claim-bearing scrolls vs 525 registry entries; sections 1.4.x and area 1.5.3 stand as the post-Paper-015 drawing, accurate as of that drawing. The next drawing must also refresh extract_registry.py's EXPECTED_SHA and re-prove in Lean. Canonical research.phext after the study: 544 scrolls, sha256 e0b3f286450b4defb8cc3eea5c2b122b4d0abfb053f1f47a8ea729176f80932c; Singular audit residual 0; index regenerated.
