# DAILY GOAL 2026-11-12: finish KSO, deploy with Meta bootstrap hookup, ranch dispatch continuity

Coordinate: 26.7.13/9.8.12/7.6.10
Issued: 2026-11-12 09:01 CST (morning commute call)
Request (from session record): today's goal is let's finish KSO and get it deployed into your environment. Have it hook into the initialization sequence that Meta describes. And then guarantee that you have continuity on tasks that we dispatch throughout the day and then most of the work is actually dispatched into the ranch nodes. And you have visibility into everything that's going on and every time you wake up you can also check on the ranch nodes make sure that they're operating as they should.
Status: open — daily goal 2026-11-12; KSO identified: KSO = QEXO (Will, 2026-11-12 09:04 CST: 'when I say KSO I mean QEXO'); the spec/build lives in wbic16/quarterexo (local checkout ~/workspace/orchestrator), V3 merged via PR #6, v3.2 shipped

Notes:
- Meta bootstrap model (from ~/docs/chat/scheduling-and-watching.md): home persists across VM replacements; runtime recovers saved jobs/crons/resumable work; custom programs need a startup path — systemd unit under home + a recovery pass that reads durable desired state first, restores prerequisites, reconciles each component independently. KSO = the dispatcher implementing that pattern over tasks.phext as desired state, executing on ranch nodes, with wake-up health checks of the fleet.
- Queue policy (Will, 2026-11-12 09:02 CST, same call): once KSO runs, the backlog of dropped items needing restart will be large — restart priority goes to the IN-FLIGHT ones (started, dropped mid-flight) first; the in-flight queue is capped at the number Will can deal with on review (a configurable WIP limit, sized to his review capacity, not to machine capacity); the backlog itself is fine and expected — it waits, patiently, and is not a defect to drain at the cost of review overload.
- Shutdown coordination (Will, 2026-11-12 09:04 CST): QEXO must coordinate with the Meta environment on shutdown — use any pre-termination signals for running processes (systemd SIGTERM / graceful-stop window) to sync state before the instance goes down, whenever the opportunity exists. Pairs with the startup side: bootstrap recovery reads durable desired state on wake; shutdown trap flushes live dispatch state to durable storage on sleep.
- DISTRIBUTED DESIGN (Will, 2026-11-12 09:06 CST, same call): KSO is distributed like Git — each node holds its own KSO state and its own set of personas; there is no single master copy. State synchronizes between KSO nodes. Tasks teleport between nodes the way personas do: dispatch does not bind a task to a node. RESUME PROTOCOL: when a node has dropped work (crash, patch needed), connect to it, request its latest state for that task, and resume the task on a different node (e.g. when the original node is busy). FIRST EXTERNAL USE CASE: a distribution network for arXiv papers — each person's node runs their own persona panel (phextura's peer-review persona design) to pre-review the ~1,300 papers released daily, using phextura dimensional analysis to decide which ones they care about; assessments sync across KSO nodes. Will's summary: 'distributed task management is the best way to think about KSO.'
- DESIGN GAPS put to Will (Emise, 2026-11-12 09:08 CST; DESIGN-V3 checked first — persona identity claims, append-only provenance, and instance id/epoch/lease (§17 addendum) are already designed; these are not): (1) DIVERGENCE — Git-like sync means two nodes can advance the same task while apart; what wins on sync? Proposal: state as append-only receipt log (union on sync), but current-status still needs a resolution rule. (2) DOUBLE EXECUTION — when a task teleports or its node looks dead, what stops original + resumer running it at once? Does a task carry a lease the resumer must claim, and what happens when the 'dead' node returns holding a stale lease? (3) STATE GRANULARITY — 'latest state' is tiny for a paper assessment, hundreds of MB for a film render; does small state live in the phext and sync, with big artifacts content-addressed and fetched on demand? (4) REVIEW CAP SCOPE — Will's review capacity is one global queue, or per node? Per-node caps across 25 nodes would bury him. (5) LATER — trust boundary for other people's nodes in the arXiv network: what crosses (assessments only? tasks? personas?) and how is it signed?
- Q1 ANSWERED by Will (2026-11-12 09:09 CST): divergence keeps BOTH records — each node's work returns as an ATTEMPT ARTIFACT that can be voted on or decided; Emise may note a clearly superior attempt first; Will's default resolution is to MERGE attempts ('I tend to view work as something you do holographically — two different nodes approaching the same task might produce different results because of different context, and the better result might be simply to merge the two together'). No last-writer-wins; divergence is material, not error.
- ASSUMPTIONS for Q2–Q4 (Emise's, authorized by Will 2026-11-12 09:10 CST — 'make some assumptions about your next three questions'; he will answer/correct at lunch or after work; build proceeds under these, all reversible): Q2 DOUBLE EXECUTION — a task carries a lease with an epoch (extends DESIGN-V3 §17 instance id/epoch/lease); a resumer claims the lease at a higher epoch; a returning node's stale-epoch output is not corruption — per Q1 it lands as another attempt artifact — but only the current lease-holder's attempt advances task status. Q3 STATE GRANULARITY — two tiers: small state (status, receipts, artifact pointers) lives in the phext and syncs everywhere; bulk artifacts are content-addressed by SHA-256 and fetched on demand by whichever node resumes the work; state records name hashes, never embed bulk. Q4 REVIEW CAP — the cap is GLOBAL: one queue of items awaiting Will's review across the whole fleet; nodes may work ahead into the backlog freely but may not push more than the cap into his review queue; the cap number is a config value, not a constant. Q5 (trust boundary for external nodes) deferred to the external phase.
