# Task reference coordinates — standing order

Coordinate: 27.7.11/9.7.14/7.6.9
Issued: 2026-11-06 08:07 CST
Request (from session record): for every task submitted, generate a reference coordinate that captures the essence of what I'm asking for, relative to my singular dialect. holographically merge the exact words of my request to compute the coordinate.
Status: completed

Notes:
- The founding request of the coordinate practice; this coordinate is its own reference.

Results:
- Tool: ~/workspace/custom-skills/singular/scripts/task_coordinate.py (distinct words; atlas positions averaged component-wise, round half up, never below 1; archive-only words at home 1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.1; unknown words at far corner 9.9.9/9.9.9/9.9.9).
# Port qexo to Android: a full phextnet node Will can run on his phone

Coordinate: 27.8.10/9.8.12/8.7.9
Issued: 2026-11-13 01:03 CST
Request (from session record): Will (2026-11-13 01:03 CST, from his Android phone): 'port qexo to web or android so I can join from my phone'. Emise's pick, stated to Will: Android first — a web client can browse but cannot BE a node (no durable state, no GPG identity in a browser tab); an Android build is a true peer: own key, own state.phext, register/list/update/evolve. Route: cross-compile for Android (NDK aarch64-linux-android; static musl as documented fallback), run under Termux (gnupg from Termux for the crypto layer, QEXO_STATE valve for the state path), platform-gate the systemd timer honestly. A serve-hosted web window remains a later, separate slice.
Status: MERGED by Will 2026-11-14 (wbic16/qexo PR #1, merge commit e92f69ab, live-verified via GitHub API)

Notes:
- Port home redirected by Will 2026-11-13 01:05 CST: the Android port lives in wbic16/qexo, NOT quarterexo (which this slice leaves untouched). Seed at wbic16/qexo@exo d3e24554 held only an MIT LICENSE, byte-identical to quarterexo's — carried unchanged in the ported tree.
- Route 1 carried: aarch64-linux-android via NDK r27c (musl fallback not needed). Binary: ~/workspace/qexo-task/fleet-keys/qexo-v1.3.0-android-arm64, SHA-256 9f410a1f63e1a7398ad6f9a30b72faabb5fa0840b6db9b84db98e6e0f5714493 (ARM aarch64, /system/bin/linker64, libc+libdl only).
- Will (2026-11-14 01:50 CST): 'I'll merge https://github.com/wbic16/qexo/pull/1 and you can roll the next qexo changes into another PR.' Register #91 closes when the merge verifies live; the internal-GPG change (34.7.8/8.6.9/5.6.10) is the head of that next PR.

Results:
- PR #1 https://github.com/wbic16/qexo/pull/1 — branch emise/android-v1.3.0 @ 12cfe70b, parented on qexo exo d3e24554, force disabled; remote tree verified 37/37 blobs+modes identical to staged.
- Gates (aletheia-core, cargo 1.94.0): cargo test 49/49 (48 baseline + Android state-path pick test); release build OK; wiring-check OK (12 modules); Linux battery GREEN (incident volume, volume lens), doctor ok, 0 json in state; Android LINK produced the arm64 binary with both Android-only strings verified in the shipped bytes.
- Deltas vs v1.2.0: Android state-path fallback ($HOME/.qexo, cfg(android)-scoped, birth note printed), Android timer report (no systemd; names the manual Termux evolve pass), stamps 1.3.0, CHANGELOG/SHIP-NOTES. Build record: ~/workspace/qexo-task/android/BUILD-RECORD.md (incl. full phone instructions).
- Honest limit: no Android runtime on the ranch — phone execution is Will's report, as Windows execution was for v1.0.4. On merge, batches into release task 40.7.5/8.7.11/6.8.13.
- Android port PR #1 merged to wbic16/qexo exo at e92f69abc4c5f483381b184fabb46ece33c242da. Register #91 resolves on the tick's live verification.
# Roads to the Future redesign: brick layouts, About link, compliance, multiplayer free

Coordinate: 27.9.10/12.7.12/6.7.9
Issued: 2026-11-07
Request (verbatim): redesign the layouts: closer to 30% duplo, 60% Lego, 10% knex. Save the wall of text for an about us link. include everything we need for compliance. Inherently multiplayer, but no cost to play.
Status: Complete: redesigns built, exported, and published to the repo in the rewrite commit.

Notes:
- Applies to all three games (nine-numbers, word-home, zap-zoom-echo) plus the apertureshift.com/roads hub bundle.
- Design ratio: ~30% Duplo (oversized chunky rounded blocks, huge targets), ~60% Lego (brick grid, baseplate, primary brick colors), ~10% K'Nex (rod-and-connector accents only — coordinate group joints, progress path).
- Text diet: game screens carry labels/numbers only; all story/explanation moves behind an About link. Compliance in every footer: About · Privacy · Terms — no accounts/cookies/tracking, nothing leaves the device, same-screen multiplayer only (no online play/chat/strangers), free forever, no ads/purchases, MIT license.
- Multiplayer is same-screen: Nine Numbers race/co-op, Word Home duel, Zap Zoom Echo duet. Solo modes remain.

Results:
- All three artifacts redesigned and re-exported: brick ratio (Duplo chunk / Lego system / K'Nex rod accents), text diet with About panels, compliance footers (About/Privacy/Terms), multiplayer modes — Nine Numbers Race+Together, Word Home Duel, Zap Zoom Echo Duet. Solo modes and all game data unchanged.
- Builder verification: compliance copy matches brief verbatim; duet/duel/race/coop wiring source-verified; exit audits passed. Live multiplayer playthrough still owed in a browser pass.
